Chapter: Codex Entry
  grounds Device.Port, Device.Block
  cites Codex chapter Opening (codex-opening)

 The entry point, and nothing else.

 `opening` is resolved BY NAME all over the compiler -- `opening-entry-point`
 in Core/Name.codex is the literal "opening", the emitter calls it
 (X86_64Chapter.codex:456), the scoper counts definitions of it
 (ChapterScoper.codex:46) and refuses a second, and it heads both emit-root
 lists. Keeping that name here and renaming the driver body to `codex-opening`
 leaves every one of those lookups working while freeing the chapter that
 holds the phases: a program that is its OWN entry point can now bundle
 Chapter: Opening and call compile-checked directly, because that chapter no
 longer defines an `opening` to collide with its own.

 This file is a LOCAL REPRODUCTION of the shape upstream described on PR 116,
 written to measure whether it gives the ladder what it needs before their
 version ships. It is not their code and must not be sent anywhere.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, FileSystem, Device.Block] Nothing = act
   codex-opening
  end

